  "apod": {
    "date": "2026-11-02",
    "title": "A Long Cloud on Mars",
    "explanation": " Explanation:  What’s causing this unusually long cloud on  Mars ? Known as the  Arsia Mons Elongated Cloud  (AMEC) and first recorded by NASA’s  Viking Orbiter 2  in 1977, the  strange cloud  has been imaged in greater detail more recently by  ESA ’s robotic  Mars Express  spacecraft. The  featured   image  is from 2018. The  cloud forms  only during  spring  and summer  seasons  and then only during morning hours. Made of water-ice, the most familiar process that causes  cloud crystals  to  form around bits of dust  would fail due to the thinness and lack of dust high in the  Martian atmosphere .  Leading speculation  holds that dust is not involved. After the cloud forms near the volcano  Arsia Mons , wind blows the cloud  downstream  — creating a tail that can extend over 1500 kilometers. Efforts to better understand  AMEC s will continue, an understanding that may bolster a  more complete view  of  cloud formation  on  Earth .   Tomorrow's picture:  eyeing a cat",
    "image": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/MonsCloud_MarsExpress_1920.jpg?w=1920&h=1200&fit=clip&crop=faces,focalpoint",
    "link": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-2-a-long-cloud-on-mars/",
    "credit": " ESA ,  DLR ,  FU Berlin ;  Processing &  License :  J. Cowart ",
    "alt": "Mars is seen from orbit with an orange landscape and two prominent volcanoes on the right. Extending  horizontally from the nearer volcano is a very long white cloud.",
    "media_type": "image"
  },
  "updated": "2026-11-02T08:50:45.488040+00:00"
